[gd_scene load_steps=19 format=3]

; CLUSTER LAUNCHER — six-chamber revolver, fires on an arc.
;
; Same axes and scale as the Mark One and the Squad Automatic (barrel down +X,
; about five units long, Holder scale 0.26), and the SAME IRON SIGHTS: rear
; aperture y 0.473–0.673 centred on 0.573, front post tip 0.573, flat. Three
; weapons, one sight picture. bolt_rifle_model.tscn's header says why flat.
;
; THE DRUM IS SIZED TO THE SIGHT LINE, NOT THE OTHER WAY ROUND. It started at
; radius 0.78, which at the Holder's 0.26 is a metre and a half across — and
; it stood 3.7 degrees ABOVE the line of sight, so aiming down the irons showed
; you the top of your own drum and nothing else. At 0.46 it is about 24cm, the
; size a 40mm revolver actually is, and its crown clears the sight line by 0.17.
; Everything between the two sights is now under y 0.41.
;
; THE REAR SIGHT IS A FIXED HOLD-OVER RETICLE, marked 25 / 50 / 75 / 100.
; No moving parts and no key to press: every mark is live at once. Put the
; target on the mark for its range and the shell lands there. Because raising
; the muzzle drops the target in the frame, THE MARKS RUN DOWNWARD AS RANGE
; INCREASES — 25m on top, 100m at the bottom.
;
; WHICH CONVENTION THIS IS, AND WHY IT MATTERS. A real ladder sight aligns
; mark -> FRONT POST -> target, so the post is the pivot and the mark spacing
; gets multiplied by sight_radius / eye_relief, here 2.9x. Built that way these
; four marks spanned 20 degrees and the sight was a tower. A hold-over reticle
; puts the aim point ON the mark, no pivot, so the spacing is the raw
; difference in launch angle: 2.3 degrees per 25m step, seven degrees for the
; whole set. Same ballistics, a quarter of the size, and the player reads it
; without having to also find the post.
;
;   mark screen angle = elevation(50m) - elevation(range)
;   elevation(R)      = asin(R * g / v^2) / 2          (v = 55 m/s, g = 9.8)
;   mark model y      = y(50m) + 1.07268 * tan(screen angle)
;
;   range   elevation   on screen   mark y
;    25 m    2.3228      +2.34 deg   1.096540
;    50 m    4.6610       0.00       1.052743   <- the zero, on the crosshair
;    75 m    7.0311      -2.37       1.008347
;   100 m    9.4515      -4.79       0.962837
;
; Verified by firing, not by drawing: tools/preview_gl_ranges.gd takes the bore
; off the posed muzzle node, throws a shell at launch_speed under the project's
; own gravity and reports where it lands. 22.7 / 49.4 / 74.9 / 100.2m against
; marks of 25 / 50 / 75 / 100. The 25m mark is the loose one because the shell
; spawns 0.9m in front of the eye, which is 4% of that range and none of 100m.
;
; THE SIGHT LINE SITS 0.23 HIGHER THAN A RIFLE'S, front post tip at 0.80 rather
; than 0.573, and the front blade is 0.05 thin. Both are so you can SEE the
; target at 100m: at the old height the barrel's crown covered anything more
; than 3.56 degrees below the axis and the 100m aim point is 4.79 below it, and
; at the old 0.12 thickness the front post was 1.64 degrees wide against a 2m
; target's 1.15 — the post was wider than the thing it was aimed at.
;
; THE LADDER MUST TOUCH THE GUN. A previous pass had the uprights starting at
; y 0.55 while the riser stopped at 0.451, and the whole sight floated a
; centimetre above the receiver. They now run from 0.40, inside the riser, and
; stop at 1.14 just above the 25m pair rather than running off the screen.
;
; Silhouette is deliberately nothing like the Ancient Grenade Launcher, which
; is a square box with a stubby tube: this is a drum with the rounds showing.
; Palette from machine_gun_model.tscn. No emissive.

